  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They never led, and one early mistake cost them the game.",
    "what_this_means": "Jeffrey Springs delivered five solid innings. The pitching was good enough to win. But the offense wasted its chances and left six runners on base, unable to convert runners in scoring position when it mattered. Shea Langeliers drove in three runs to keep the lineup relevant, and Brooks Kriske threw 1.2 scoreless to steady the pen after Joel Kuhnel gave up two early. None of it was enough. The Cards scored four in the first. A critical miss in the opening inning. And the Athletics never recovered from that deficit. They had two runners on with chances to answer immediately and got nothing. By the time they scraped together three runs in the second, St. Louis had already seized control. This is a team built to find edges in close games; losing by two when the pitching was solid tells you exactly what went wrong.",
    "what_to_watch": "Matthew Liberatore takes the mound tonight with a 4.07 ERA, and the Athletics need J.T. Ginn to go deeper than Springs did to avoid another early hole.",
